    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "Thanks for the report! I can reproduce this, and it is due to an integer overflow.\r\n\r\n> The bug doesn't appear when setting `max_num_parallel_iter` to an integer constant, which must mean it's somehow related to the calculation from the memory info.\r\n\r\nI think the reason you don't see it is that launches are asynchronous, and setting `max_num_parallel_iter` to a constant changed the timing of execution such that your program manages to exit before the values printed by the kernel get flushed into the terminal. I find that (and I think you should be able to reproduce too) adding `cuda.synchronize()` to the end of the program manifests the error even when there's no computation or device array:\r\n\r\n```\r\nfrom numba import cuda\r\n\r\n@cuda.jit\r\ndef bug():\r\n    i = cuda.threadIdx.x + cuda.blockIdx.x * cuda.blockDim.x\r\n    i2 = cuda.grid(1)\r\n    if i != i2:\r\n        # this shouldn't happen\r\n        print(i, i2)\r\n\r\nbug[22121216, 256]()\r\ncuda.synchronize()\r\n```",
