    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "@nathanfarlow There is a fix in PR #9235. As a workaround for now, can you write your kernels such that they don't require more than 2^31 threads, or use the manual computation?\r\n\r\nOne reason what seems like an \"obvious\" problem has slipped by for so long is that I think people generally don't use so many threads,  so this isn't a common pattern in practice. Instead, it's usually more efficient to use a grid-strided loop with fewer threads, as outlined in: https://towardsdatascience.com/cuda-by-numba-examples-1-4-e0d06651612f#0909",
